WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.106
This tutorial turns one clip into a finished edit.

2
00:00:04.106 --> 00:00:15.287
You will trim two cuts and join them with a dissolve, apply a warm colour look, add a headline, a floating badge and a lower third, and then export an MP4.

3
00:00:15.287 --> 00:00:17.450
It takes about twenty minutes.

4
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.433
Begin by importing the clip.

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:25.218
Choose Import media in the Media panel and select your file.

6
00:00:25.218 --> 00:00:28.887
Its length and thumbnail appear straight away.

7
00:00:28.887 --> 00:00:34.310
Drag the clip onto the video track, then name the project Fire edit.

8
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.928
Watch the clip once and find its best moment.

9
00:00:37.928 --> 00:00:42.837
Select the clip, and in the inspector under Timing, set Out to eight seconds.

10
00:00:42.837 --> 00:00:45.450
The first cut now runs for eight seconds.

11
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.199
Duplicate the clip with the Duplicate button in the inspector.

12
00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:52.779
Place the copy where the first cut ends.

13
00:00:52.779 --> 00:01:00.390
Set its In point to eight seconds, so the second cut shows new footage, and trim it to about seven and a half seconds.

14
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.183
Select both clips and open the Edit tab in the inspector.

15
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:07.666
Under colour, set the look to Warm.

16
00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:12.420
Everything shifts toward amber, and the highlights roll off gently.

17
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.250
If the footage is dark, raise the brightness a little.

18
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.945
Choose Add title and type Fire edit.

19
00:01:19.945 --> 00:01:27.228
Set the size to ten percent, select a bold weight, and raise the title to twenty percent.

20
00:01:27.228 --> 00:01:34.510
Extend its duration to fifteen seconds, then apply the Spring preset from the Motion tab.

21
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.793
Open the Graphics panel and insert an ellipse.

22
00:01:42.793 --> 00:01:49.665
Size it as a small circle, fill it with orange, and place it low in the frame.

23
00:01:49.665 --> 00:01:56.890
In the Motion tab, expand Animate with code, choose the Float recipe and apply it.

24
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.944
Move the playhead to two seconds.

25
00:02:00.944 --> 00:02:04.949
In the Graphics panel, open the lower third template.

26
00:02:04.949 --> 00:02:12.280
Enter a name and a supporting line, set the duration to four seconds, and add it to the timeline.

27
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.413
Open the Effects panel and find Transitions.

28
00:02:16.413 --> 00:02:19.719
Select the second clip, and apply Dissolve.

29
00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:26.409
The transition overlaps the two cuts and moves later video up, so the edit stays tight.

30
00:02:26.409 --> 00:02:30.100
It plays in the preview and survives the export.

31
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.695
Play the whole edit once from the start.

32
00:02:33.695 --> 00:02:41.450
The headline springs in, the badge floats, the lower third arrives at two seconds, and the two cuts dissolve.

33
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.153
When you are satisfied, select Export.

34
00:02:44.153 --> 00:02:50.840
The render runs on the server with FFmpeg, and the finished MP4 downloads from the same panel.

35
00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.590
That completes the fire edit.

36
00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:59.315
Every layer is an ordinary clip, so anything can be retimed or restyled afterwards.

37
00:02:59.315 --> 00:03:06.420
The documentation covers transitions, rendering and the API operations behind every step you performed.

